This page opens Part IV, 'Un épinglage canonique', in the notes on the loop equation ξ = αρ(α)^{-1}, η = βσ(β)^{-1}. The setting is the rank-2 linear and projective groups over a base scheme S, as used in the previous sections. Grothendieck recalls a standard dictionary before building a canonical pinning.

Over S, the following data are equivalent:
- a form G of GL(2)_S;
- a form SG of SL(2)_S;
- a form PG of PGL(2)_S;
- an Azumaya algebra M of rank 4 (a form of M_2(O_S));
- a P^1-bundle P → S (a Severi–Brauer scheme of relative dimension 1).

P has several interpretations:
1. The scheme of Borel subgroups B of G, SB of SG, or PB of PG. The canonical maps SG → G → PG induce bijections between the Borel subgroups of the three groups.
2. The scheme of 'borelian' additive one-parameter subgroups, i.e. those equal to the unipotent radical of some Borel subgroup. The same bijections hold for these.
3. Sections N of M that are regular (non-zero on every fibre) and satisfy det N = 0 and Tr N = 0, equivalently N^2 = 0. These are the 'strictly nilpotent' sections. Each such N gives a borelian one-parameter subgroup of SG, namely the image of G_a under λ ↦ exp(λN) = 1 + λN.

The next page completes this. N and aN with a a unit give the same subgroup. So such subgroups correspond to sections of the projectivisation of the trace-zero part fM of M, lying on the conic det N = 0. It also passes to unipotent elements e = 1 + N, characterised by det e = 1, Tr e = 2.

The purpose is evidently to produce a canonical pinning (épinglage) of the rank-2 group attached to the loop-equation data. This would let the earlier calculations with ξ, η be carried out intrinsically, presumably also in the generalisation with a sign ε raised on the previous page.
